            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"The shadowy realm of the dead, the underworld, or the grave, symbolizing mortality, the finality of physical death, and separation from the land of the living.\",\"primary_basis\": [\"Psalm 16:10\",\"Psalm 139:8\",\"Isaiah 14:9-11\",\"Job 10:21-22\",\"Ecclesiastes 9:10\"],\"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"A place of conscious torment and punishment for the wicked, historically treated as synonymous with Hell or Gehenna in older traditional theology.\",\"basis\": [\"Psalm 9:17\",\"Luke 16:23\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"},{\"meaning\": \"A bipartite temporary holding place containing both a realm of comfort for the righteous (Abraham's Bosom) and a realm of torment for the wicked, prior to Christ's resurrection.\",\"basis\": [\"Luke 16:22-26\",\"Ephesians 4:8-9\",\"1 Peter 3:19\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"},{\"meaning\": \"A strictly literal reference to the physical dirt grave, representing a state of unconsciousness, non-existence, or 'soul sleep' until the final resurrection.\",\"basis\": [\"Ecclesiastes 9:5\",\"Psalm 6:5\",\"Psalm 115:17\"],\"prominence\": \"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\": true,\"confidence\": \"high\",\"notes\": \"While modern biblical scholarship holds a settled consensus that Sheol refers to the ancient Israelite concept of the underworld or the grave rather than a place of eternal torment, historic Christian interpretation frequently conflated Sheol with Gehenna (Hell) or Hades. This conflation resulted in varied theological traditions regarding the conscious state of the dead and the Harrowing of Hell.\"}",
